  Thomas Hobbes argue that when society  exists in a state of nature registers  say no order government whatsoever that  people would basically kill steal and  enslave others in order to ensure their  own survival and their own safety and  they would be allowed to it's the only  natural right that individuals high to  do whatever it is they need to do in  order to stay alive  but because this would be an exceedingly  harsh and brutal environment this would  inevitably lead to what he referred to  as a perpetual and permanent war ball  against all and so in order to ensure  that society itself did not devolve into  this constant state of war that people  would sacrifice this natural right to do  whatever they needed to do in order to  live and create a government which in  turn would come with a set of rules that  you  can't steal you can't kill someone and  so forth a place for them to air their  grievances which he referred to as the  Leviathan however if you look at someone  like John Locke John Locke argued that  in a state of nature the rights of men  and women were not limited to doing  whatever it was they needed to do in  order to survive instead that the rights  of individuals were inalienable that  people had the right to life liberty and  property something that would be carried  over in future philosophers and even the  framers of the US Constitution the  result was that in order to ensure that  these rights were maintained that  individuals would form an organized  government to protect these various  rights but regardless of how you slice  it that coming together of an organized  society and the formation of a  government for that society is designed  to maintain and to ensure the survival  of the rights and the lives of the  people involved it's the reason why we  create laws that say it's illegal to  steal not because we morally believe  that stealing is wrong  but because if stealing is illegal it  means you cannot steal from me and if  you do I have legal recourse for that  and so this takes us into the second  role that once this social contract is  established and what's this organized  society it's on its way we create  systems by which this social contract  can be enforced we create judges we  create police officers individuals that  we can go to in order to air a grievance  with regards to a violation of this  social contract and in turn for those  individuals who did violate this social  contract the third role comes into play  and that we provide punishments for  those who violate this contract either  they're sentenced to jail or depending  on the severity of the crime committed  we may even take their lives by virtue  of execution and so the question becomes  this if social contracts or laws as  they're more commonly referred to you  and the resulting government are formed  managed and enforced by society then  what need is there for superheroes why  not simply continue to rely on police  and judges to ensure that those who  violate the laws are punished adequately  to answer this we have to ask what do  superheroes actually do and are the  tasks that they undertake beyond the  ability for society to handle on its own  on a surface level superheroes fight bad  guys it's the most fundamental aspect of  who they are sometimes superheroes fight  villains who have a kind of  technological advantage over Society  maybe they're smarter than everybody  else maybe they have some giant warship  that they use to rain down fire on a  city other  time's superheroes fight villains who  possess a level of power this society  simply isn't prepared to face off  against somebody like magneto who can  manipulate and control the entire  electromagnetic spectrum or somebody  like copycat who can become anyone and  even duplicate the powers of others but  regardless superheroes fight villains  who if the superheroes did not exist  would be able to successfully conquer  all of society or even destroy society  itself using 